// GENERATED (each mark stays the trademark of its owner). Self-hosted as path
// data, so a job row or the landing wall sends no visitor request off
// offerfy.cc and there is no logo CDN to rot.
//
// Most marks come from Simple Icons, which is CC0. Simple Icons covers the
// well-known brands and nothing else: checked against 16.37.0 on 2026-11-15,
// it had NO symbol for any of the 23 employers that were still wordmarks, so
// do not expect a bump of that package to close the gap. Eighteen of those 23
// were filled from three other sources, in this order of preference:
//
//   1. @lobehub/icons-static-svg (MIT)  -- Cerebras, Cohere, Together AI
//   2. Wikimedia Commons, PD-textlogo   -- Anduril, Applied Materials, Marvell
//   3. the employer's own brand file    -- the rest; provenance per mark is
//      listed below
//
// Two traps, both of which cost a round of wrong conclusions here:
//
// A Commons hit is usually the WORDMARK, not a square symbol. CrowdStrike's
// and Delivery Hero's Commons files are lettering only, with no falcon and no
// bird to lift out of them. Look at what you fetched before believing a
// company is covered.
//
// A 403 on the HTML does NOT mean the brand art is unreachable. Dcard answers
// 403 on every page yet serves /favicon.ico as a 256px app mark; Affirm and
// Appier do the same. Always probe /favicon.ico and /favicon.svg directly
// before concluding a site is closed -- and note that a file served as .ico
// may actually be a PNG, so sniff it rather than trusting the extension.
//
// Five employers are still wordmarks, for two different reasons. Chime and
// Delivery Hero return 403 to everything including the favicon. CrowdStrike,
// Klaviyo and Wing answer fine but publish no square symbol we could find:
// CrowdStrike's falcon is nowhere in its own markup, Klaviyo's pages carry
// only its customers' logos, and Wing's 64px icon bakes in a drop shadow that
// a silhouette trace turns into a staircase.
//
// Where each of the eighteen came from, 2026-11-15:
//
//   Affirm             affirm.com favicon (square app mark)
//   Anduril            Wikimedia Commons, public domain (PD-textlogo)
//   Appier             appier.com favicon (square app mark)
//   Applied Materials  Wikimedia Commons, public domain (PD-textlogo)
//   Cerebras           @lobehub/icons-static-svg, MIT
//   Cohere             @lobehub/icons-static-svg, MIT
//   Dcard              dcard.tw favicon (square app mark)
//   Lalamove           lalamove.com favicon
//   Marvell            Wikimedia Commons, public domain (PD-textlogo)
//   Mercury            mercury.com favicon.svg
//   Micron             micron.com apple-icon-180
//   Pinkoi             pinkoi.com inline app mark
//   Plaid              plaid.com safari-pinned-tab.svg
//   Ramp               ramp.com apple-touch-icon
//   Scale AI           scale.com favicon.svg
//   Together AI        @lobehub/icons-static-svg, MIT
//   Waymo              waymo.com inline brand mark
//   Workday            workday.com favicon (the w-and-arc mark)
//
// The seven traced from a raster (Affirm, Appier, Dcard, Lalamove, Micron,
// Ramp, Workday) came off an app
// icon, which is the only square art those three publish; the rest are
// vector all the way down.
//
// Keyed by the employer name exactly as the board files spell it, which is
// also what Job.company carries -- that string is the join between a posting
// and its mark, so renaming a board's company means renaming the key here.
// companies.test.ts holds both to the board files.
//
// `hex` is the brand colour, or null when it cannot survive both themes: a
// near-black mark (Anthropic, Vercel) disappears on dark paper and an acid
// one (Robinhood's lime) disappears on light. Those take --ink and flip with
// the theme instead.
//
// The test is contrast against BOTH papers (--paper, light and dark), at
// least 1.35:1 each, applied at generation time. Plain relative luminance
// was tried first and was wrong: a saturated red or purple (Pinterest,
// Datadog, Nubank) has a low luminance while being perfectly legible on
// either paper, and nulling those threw away most of the wall's colour.
/**
 * `fillRule` is "evenodd" when the mark is a silhouette with a counter cut
 * out of it -- Pinkoi's P inside its disc, Ramp's swoosh inside its circle.
 * Simple Icons winds its subpaths for the default nonzero rule and so needs
 * no flag; a mark traced or lifted from a brand file generally does.
 */
export type EmployerMark = { d: string; hex: string | null; fillRule?: "evenodd" };

